course: dictionary
lesson: 2107
title: Contrast
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Contrast means a clear difference between two things, especially when you put them side by side. «Her red coat contrasted with the white snow.» The red coat looks very different from the snow, so contrasted means showed a clear difference.
explain: As a verb, to contrast means to compare two things to show how they are different. In English you may be asked to compare and contrast: say how things are alike, and then say how they are different. Words like «but», «however» and «whereas» are signal words that show a contrast.
explain: Contrast also means how far the light and dark parts of a picture stand apart. Painters know that real light can be much brighter than paint, so they put darker colours beside a sky to make it look bright. When you read, ask yourself: is this sentence showing a difference, or a likeness?
fact: Contrast means a clear difference between two things, especially when they are side by side.
fact: «Compare and contrast» means say how things are alike and how they are different.
fact: Words of contrast include «but», «however» and «whereas».
fact: In art and on screens, contrast is the difference between the light and dark parts of a picture.
fact: A sharp contrast is a very big difference between two things.
exercise: «Her red coat contrasted with the white snow.» What does contrasted mean here? | Hid in the snow | Was the same colour as the snow | *Showed a clear difference | Melted the snow
exercise: In English, what does «compare and contrast» ask you to do? | Copy out a long list of facts | Read two books and pick your favourite | *Say how things are alike and how they are different | Draw a picture of two things
exercise: Which word is a word of contrast? | *However | And | Because | So
test: Which word shows a contrast in this sentence? «The sun was bright, but the wind was cold.» | Because | And | *But | So
test: In a painting, what does contrast mean? | The size of the picture | *How far the light and dark parts stand apart | How old the painting is | The colour of the frame
exam: Which sentence shows a contrast? | Tom likes apples and Sam likes pears. | *Tom likes apples, but Sam likes pears. | Tom likes apples, so he ate one. | Tom likes apples because he is hungry.
